> > 1. smtprelay refuses a sender or recipient with 451, not 5xx. The
> >    code is hard-coded in senderChecker and recipientChecker in
> >    main.go; there is no setting.

Q1. The spec promises a 5xx, and you called recipient filtering a
key design constraint, so I won't weaken it on my own. The options:

  a. The spec says a refused sender or recipient gets a 4xx reply.
     msmtp still exits non-zero and nothing is sent. The cost is that
     4xx means "try again later", which misleads anything that reads
     the code, though no client here queues.
  b. lab-mail carries a local patch so smtprelay answers 550. That
     means building smtprelay from ports in the jail rather than
     installing the package, and keeping the patch current.
  c. Another relay. None of the alternatives the spec weighs has both
     a recipient allowlist and pass-through of Fastmail's reply.

I recommend (a). Separately, should I offer smtprelay upstream a patch
to answer 550? That is a pull request on GitHub, so I would not send
it without your yes.

> > 3. smtprelay adds a Received header to each message before relaying
> >    it. The spec says it does not rewrite the headers.

Q2. You asked for no header rewriting. Is it acceptable for the spec
to say that smtprelay adds one Received line, as every MTA does, and
changes nothing else in the message? I recommend yes.

> > 4. Dovecot serves an empty INBOX next to lists. IMAP reserves INBOX
> >    as every user's primary mailbox, and Dovecot requires an inbox
> >    namespace.

Q3. The options:

  a. The list is the mailbox lists, and an empty, read-only INBOX sits
     beside it.
  b. The list is served as INBOX, and there is no mailbox named lists.
     Dovecot's config is simpler, and no mailbox is empty.

I recommend (a). The name says what the mailbox holds, it matches the
Fastmail folder, and a second mirrored folder could sit beside it.

> > 5. mbsync creates mail 0600 and folders 0700, hard-coded in
> >    drv_maildir.c, whatever the umask. So vmail could not read the
> >    maildir at all.

Q4. The spec says Dovecot's user cannot write the maildir. It does not
say how that user reads it. The options:

  a. The fetch job opens up permissions after each pull. For the few
     seconds between mbsync writing a message and the chmod, IMAP
     cannot read that message. A client fetch that lands in that
     moment fails and succeeds on its next run.
  b. Dovecot runs as mbsync's user. Read-only then rests only on
     Dovecot's ACL. This weakens the spec's guarantee.
  c. The maildir is its own ZFS dataset with an inherited ACL that
     grants Dovecot's user read access. I infer, but have not tested,
     that this needs aclinherit=passthrough on that dataset. I cannot
     see beastie's datasets from lab-claude.

I recommend (a), because it works on any filesystem. If you prefer
(c), tell me whether /jails/data/lab-mail/mail is its own dataset and
whether you will set its ACL properties, or whether the instance
should.

> > 6. The secrets directory holds the Fastmail login as well as the app
> >    password, in two files, user and password.

Q5. The login is not secret, but it is your account name. Should it
live in the secrets directory next to the password, or in lab-mail's
config in the repo? I recommend the secrets directory, so the repo
does not name your account and both values are in one place for the
operator.

> > 7. The spec calls the Fastmail folder "lists/". mbsyncrc uses the
